  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Friday...\n\nAs Yesterday's vort max exists to our east a high amplitude ridge\nwill build into the intermountain west. Even through the main \nenergy has existed into the great plains, a large upper level low \nremains dominant centered over MB/Western Ontario. As this feature\nlags in its eastward progression, one more localized vort max may\npivot around through far NE MT and Western ND. This could give \nsome scattered snow showers to areas near Baker this afternoon \nwith all accumulations remaining very light. All other locations \nwill see mostly sunny conditions today with the exception of the\nisolated snow shower in the mountains. Associated with this weak \nfeature, 850mb winds are forecast to be around 30kts, and 700mb \nwinds in the low 40s kt, over Eastern MT. With a well mixed \natmosphere during the day today, breezy conditions over the \neastern 1/2 of the CWA can be expected. Wind gusts should remain \nunder 40 mph. \n\nAs this dominat ridge continues to expand its influence through\nthe weekend and into early next week, temperatures will continue\nto rise each day. With near average highs forecast today, the\nforecast highs gradually increase each day through Tuesday. There\nis a 90% chance that high temperatures exceed 80 F for Billings, \nMiles City, and Sheridan on Tuesday. Along with these high \ntemperatures, also expect dry conditions with widespread Min RH \nvalues below 20% for the first half of next week.\n\nLooking ahead to the end of next week there is slowly growing \nconfidence in an impactful weather system for western N. America. \nAll clusters are now agreeing on some sort of upper level trough \nmoving through the intermountain west. Details remain uncertain at\nthis time however it does appear that this could be a good \nprecipitation event for our area. There is roughly a 50% chance \nthat Billings and Sheridan could see 0.5\" of QPF with this event. \nAs with all long range forecasts, details will change and become \nmore clear as we get closer to the event. WMR",
